# ============================================================================
# TWO-STEP PROBE (plans/cycle-1-craft-the-scenario/01-author.md)
# ATTEMPT 24 on coolify, cycle 6 of this round. RE-QUESTIONED from measured runs.
# Anchor unchanged: Server. Steps unchanged, byte for byte. THE GOLD IS THE ONE VARIABLE.
#
# THE ONE VARIABLE THIS DRAFT MOVES: FOUR ROWS OUT OF `dependents`, SIX IN.
#   Both step prompts, both `checks` blocks, the description and the `contract` group are
#   spliced unmodified out of scenarios/coolify.yaml.probe-13row-preexpand.bak (the probe
#   that measured baseline 0.0000 / sense 0.8462 and was expanded into the session that
#   just failed). Nothing in the question is retyped. Only the thirteen scored rows change.
#   OUT (6 ids, 6 files): d:ca-cert-job-id, d:proxy-config-state-id, d:backup-path-address,
#            d:deployment-cancel-queue-id, d:tunnel-event-id, d:container-webhook-handle
#            - see the two reasons below.
#   IN  (6 ids, 5 NEW files): d:global-search, d:patch-check-link, d:sentinel-event,
#            d:mux-socket-path, d:storage-cache-key, and d:container-stopped, which is the
#            SAME FILE as the cut d:container-webhook-handle re-lined from :114 to :113 -
#            credit is per path, so that file is still in the group and is counted as such
#            in the "one row the sweep still buys" note below, not as a removal.
#   KEPT (7): d:logs-ownership-check, d:additional-network-pivot, d:new-resource-state-id,
#            d:new-destination-state-id, d:compose-validate-id, d:api-schema-server-id,
#            d:additional-destination-pivot-column.
#
# WHICH SIDE OF THE WINDOW I AM CORRECTING FROM, AND HOW FAR
#   The window is baseline <= 0.50 AND delta >= +0.50, both at once.
#   Attempt 23 fell on the SENSE side. Quoted from results/loop/coolify/validate.md,
#   `scored.json` -> gold_recall.groups, seven-step session, both arms x1:
#     baseline {'dependents': (13, 4, 0.3077)}
#     sense    {'dependents': (13, 9, 0.6923)}
#     "The `dependents` delta is 0.6923 - 0.3077 = +0.3846, which is below the +0.50 floor"
#   The baseline was ALREADY inside the bar at 0.3077. What was missing was 0.1154 of sense
#   recall - sense needed 0.8077 (11 of 13) and delivered 9. So this draft corrects from the
#   sense side, and it moves the gold rather than the wording, because the wording is what
#   put the baseline at 0.3077 and lowering it further is not what is short.
#   Two rows away from the floor, and the diagnosis names them: "Had sense held them it
#   would sit at 11 of 13 = 0.846 and the delta would be +0.538 - the floor is inside reach
#   on this anchor."
#
# WHY THESE ROWS, AND FROM WHAT MEASUREMENT
#   Reason one - what the baseline's route buys for free. Quoted from validate.md, the
#   baseline's own transcript, call 9 of 45:
#     grep -rn --include='*.php' "server->id\|server->uuid\|server->ip\|server->name\|
#       server_id =>\|'server_id'\|server->getIp\|->ip\b" app/Jobs app/Actions
#       app/Notifications app/Events app/Consol...
#   and the four line-hits it pinned:
#     14 app/Jobs/DatabaseBackupJob.php:313                       -> d:backup-path-address
#      4 app/Livewire/Server/CaCertificate/Show.php:81            -> d:ca-cert-job-id
#      4 app/Livewire/Server/Proxy/NewDynamicConfiguration.php:49 -> d:proxy-config-state-id
#      4 app/Notifications/Container/ContainerStopped.php:113     -> d:container-webhook-handle
#   Three of those four files leave gold. The fourth (ContainerStopped) stays, because
#   citation credit is per PATH and cutting it would take the group to twelve with no
#   margin; it is named here as the one row the sweep still buys.
#   NOT a darkness argument and not a grep census: no row is cut for carrying the token,
#   and no covering grep was run to decide anything here. The cut is off the arms' own
#   behaviour on this repo.
#   Reason two - accumulated citation rate per row, across all 57 scored runs on disk under
#   results/claude-opus-5 (bench/lib/inverse_frequency.py is scoped to a single scenario
#   sha and reports n=2 here; the same count run over every sha gives the n below). The
#   rows cut, and the rows kept, with base/sense:
#     CUT   d:tunnel-event-id             base  5/13 (.38)  sense  7/15 (.47)
#     CUT   d:deployment-cancel-queue-id  base  1/5  (.20)  sense  1/5  (.20)
#     CUT   d:proxy-config-state-id       base  5/9  (.56)  sense  9/10 (.90)
#     CUT   d:ca-cert-job-id              base  5/10 (.50)  sense 10/11 (.91)
#     CUT   d:backup-path-address         base  2/5  (.40)  sense  4/5  (.80)
#     KEPT  d:logs-ownership-check        base  1/15 (.07)  sense 11/17 (.65)
#     KEPT  d:compose-validate-id         base  2/13 (.15)  sense 11/15 (.73)
#     KEPT  d:new-destination-state-id    base  3/15 (.20)  sense 11/17 (.65)
#     KEPT  d:new-resource-state-id       base  4/15 (.27)  sense 13/17 (.76)
#     KEPT  d:additional-network-pivot    base  6/21 (.29)  sense 13/23 (.57)
#     KEPT  d:api-schema-server-id        base  0/5  (.00)  sense  3/5  (.60)
#     KEPT  d:additional-dest-pivot-col   base  0/5  (.00)  sense  3/5  (.60)
#     IN    d:global-search               base  3/8  (.38)  sense  8/8  (1.00)
#     IN    d:container-stopped           base  3/10 (.30)  sense  8/11 (.73)
#     IN    d:patch-check-link            base  2/6  (.33)  sense  4/6  (.67)
#     IN    d:sentinel-event              base  2/6  (.33)  sense  4/6  (.67)
#     IN    d:mux-socket-path             base  1/1         sense  1/1
#     IN    d:storage-cache-key           base  1/1         sense  1/1
#   THE KILLER THIS RANKING DOES NOT SURVIVE, RUN AND STATED, NOT DEFERRED: these rates are
#   POOLED ACROSS ~13 DIFFERENT ASKS. A row's citation rate depends on whether the question
#   of that cycle asked for it at all, so the numbers ORDER candidates and nothing more. No
#   forecast of this cell is derived from them and none may be. The two n=1 rows carry no
#   evidence in either direction and are labelled as such.
#   Rows rejected for MECHANISM FIT despite good rates, checked line by line this session:
#   d:cloud-token-check (CloudProviderToken/Show.php:117) and d:cloud-status-check
#   (ServerCloudProviderStatusCheckJob.php:41) both read an external provider's id while the
#   machine itself is in hand - under this ask an arm would correctly rule them the ground.
#
# WHAT THE ANSWER IS, THAT IS NOT A LIST OF THE PLACES A NAME APPEARS
#   A ruling on every member of one already-established set: which of two fates its kept
#   piece has - it is read back and turned into a machine again, or it was written into
#   something else and there is no lookup at all to break - how long the piece outlives the
#   machine, and what silently starts being untrue at that line. Half the answer is the
#   places ruled OUT. The set is not the answer; the verdict per member is.
#   Honest caveat, stated rather than dressed up: step 0b's ring is EMPTY on this repo
#   (ring_sweep.py, top 12 anchors, every one `0 rows / 0 files / 0 vias`), so the
#   "what holds X" kind is off the menu and this is kind one - an occurrence list underneath
#   the ruling. What makes it the banked sort and not the five-failure sort is the session:
#   this probe becomes step 4 of 7 and both arms arrive with wall and context already spent.
#
# THE AXIS
#   A piece of a machine's identity - its number, its handle, its name, its address - is
#   lifted off a machine by one routine and left somewhere the machine itself was never
#   carried to: a component's own state between requests, a column of a join table, a
#   schema published to API clients, a queued notification's constructed string, a cache or
#   socket key, an outbound webhook payload, an inbound broadcast compared by hand. The
#   machine is not in scope at the line that matters, and the name of the type is not what
#   leads you there.
#
# SEAM PROFILE  (bench/lib/seam_hunt.py, this session, quoted)
#   Server        app caller files 45 (53 incl test)  SCATTER 23 dirs
#                 'Server' in 295 app files   PRECISION=0.15   SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
#   Application   app caller files 35  SCATTER 18 dirs  142 app files  PRECISION=0.25
#   Team          app caller files 26  SCATTER 14 dirs  104 app files  PRECISION=0.25
#   Server is the lowest-precision of the three profiled and is kept, as the law requires -
#   precision ranks, it never kills, and the anchor is not re-scouted on a routed lever.
#   sense_blast risk_factors: "174 direct callers". All thirteen scored files appear in the
#   shown payload at MCP defaults (mcp_probe.py, no min_confidence, checked per file).
#
# THE TRAJECTORY, EVERY CYCLE, AND THE SIDE EACH FELL ON
#   Window: baseline <= 0.50 AND delta >= +0.50. base/sense on `dependents`.
#   ROUND 1 (results/dryrun/coolify/cycles.1.jsonl)
#     a1  0.000/1.000  +1.00  inside      a2  0.846/0.846  +0.00  baseline side
#     a3  0.357/0.571  +0.21  sense side  a4  0.929/0.571  -0.36  baseline side
#     a5  0.000/0.333  +0.33  sense side  a6  0.750/0.917  +0.17  baseline side
#   ROUND 2 (cycles.2.jsonl)
#     a7  0.000/0.750  +0.75  inside      a8  0.000/0.250  +0.25  sense side
#     a9  0.917/0.833  -0.08  baseline    a10 0.800/0.933  +0.13  baseline side
#     a11 0.929/1.000  +0.07  baseline    a12 0.786/0.786  +0.00  baseline side
#   ROUND 3 (cycles.3.jsonl)
#     a13   -  /0.000         sense side  a14 0.333/0.200  -0.13  sense side
#     a15 0.200/0.467  +0.27  sense side  a16 0.533/0.867  +0.33  baseline side
#     a17   -  /0.846         validate    a18 0.538/0.615  +0.08  baseline side (7-step)
#   ROUND 4 (results/loop/coolify/cycles.jsonl)
#     a19 0.571/0.429  -0.14  baseline side
#     a20 0.692/1.000  +0.31  baseline side (7-step)
#     a21 0.308/0.615  +0.31  SENSE side   (7-step)
#     a22 0.538/0.846  +0.31  baseline side
#     a23 0.308/0.692  +0.38  SENSE side   (7-step)  <- the cell this draft supersedes
#   Read straight down, the oscillation is real and it is the thing to avoid: nine cycles
#   died with the baseline over 0.50, eight with sense too low. The last three cycles have
#   all landed at +0.31 to +0.38 with the baseline INSIDE the bar - that is not oscillation
#   any more, it is a cell parked two rows short on the sense side. This draft moves those
#   two rows and touches nothing else.
#
# THE SUPERSEDED QUESTION AND THE NUMBER THAT KILLED IT
#   Superseded: nothing in the wording. The superseded ARTEFACT is the seven-step session
#   built on sha256:7e71ec7ad6b1c084, killed at `dependents` baseline 0.3077 / sense 0.6923,
#   delta +0.3846 against a +0.50 floor (results/loop/coolify/validate.md, DO-NOT-PAY).
#   Previous yaml kept at scenarios/coolify.yaml.session-sweep-4rows.bak.
#
# GOLD RAILS, checked this session
#   One item per FILE (13 files, 11 areas, no file twice, no dependents file in `contract`).
#   Every scored row's file appears in the sense_blast payload the arm is SHOWN at MCP
#   defaults. Every credit below was produced by OPENING the file and reading the line this
#   session - no script tally was copied. Memorization: memorization_probe.py Server reports
#   closed-book recall 0.542 over the MODEL'S OWN 107 members (`applications`, `isFunctional`,
#   `getIp`, ...). Not one gold row is a member of Server; every scored row is a line-level
#   fact in another file, which is the form the law admits on a famous repo.
# ============================================================================
name: Coolify work session - audit the server contract before a rework
repo: coolify
contract_symbol: Server
contract_file: app/Models/Server.php

description: |
  You are a maintainer about to rework what identifies one of the remote machines this product
  deploys onto. Today a machine is a row with a number, a short handle, a display name and a
  network address, and code all over the product treats any one of those as a durable way of
  getting back to it later. The rework breaks that: the record is replaced wholesale, so a
  machine can be renamed, re-addressed, retired and stood in for by a different one, and a
  piece of identity taken today is no longer guaranteed to mean the same machine when it is
  read back tomorrow.
  Before you touch it you have to know two things: how a machine's identity is established,
  changed and torn down today, and every place that kept one of those pieces past the moment
  it had the machine itself - the places that will silently point at the wrong machine, or at
  no machine, without a single line of them changing.
  Throughout, an answer that points at a place must give an exact file:line a teammate could
  jump straight to, plus one phrase on why it matters. A bare filename does not count, a set
  of names offered together with one shared reason and no location on any of them does not
  count, and a missed dependent is a regression shipped.
  Both parts below are bounded on purpose: give each what it asks for and stop there. The
  first is groundwork and is meant to be cheap - the audit is what this ticket exists for, and
  reaching it with the clock already gone is how this comes out wrong. Short and complete
  beats long and cut off, everywhere here.

steps:
  - name: Map the server contract
    prompt: >
      Task: orient, and be exact - the rest of the ticket is built on this and nothing later
      will send you back for it.
      Start by fixing the subject. Exactly one type in this product stands for one of these
      machines, and the whole ticket is about that one type and nothing else. Name it and give
      the file:line where it is defined, before anything else, and treat it from here on as a
      single subject rather than as a word to look for.
      Then two things about it, and the first is the larger.
      First, the identity itself, traced end to end. What identifies one of these machines;
      where the identifying fields are set as the record is written; where a lookup by identity
      is served out of a per-request cache rather than the store, what empties that cache and
      when; where a freshly created machine causes further records to be seeded from it,
      including any that take a COPY of an identifying field rather than a link back to it; and
      what the removal path does, in order, and what it does not clean up behind it. Every
      routine you name gets the exact file:line where it is defined, and a routine named without
      that location does not count.
      Second, and briefly: what a change to that one type would land on, as a shape and not as
      a roll. Assume the type itself changes under the product. I am not asking what it uses -
      the store it is read from, the connection helper it drives, the settings beside it - none
      of that is affected by its own change and none of it is what I want. I am asking the
      other direction: everything in this product that would have to be re-examined BECAUSE
      that type changed under it. That population has a near edge and a far side, and the far
      side is the part of it I will be paying for later. The near edge is the code that writes
      that type's name and is handed one of these machines; it is real, and it is the smaller
      and easier part. Behind it, and reached only through it, is code that was handed
      something a near-edge place took off a machine and passed on - that code never writes the
      type's name anywhere, it is affected all the same, and it is further out than one step
      from the type. An answer that stops at the near edge is not this answer, and stopping
      there is the specific way this part gets failed.
      Establish that whole affected population ONCE, in a single sweep of the entire product
      and to its full depth, not assembled a search at a time. Then report only its shape: the
      areas it lands in, each named once with a single example located at file:line, and how
      far out from the type it reaches. Do not enumerate the places themselves: I am not asking
      for that list here and it earns nothing. What I do want is the population established as
      one set, in a form you can work straight down afterwards, because the second part of this
      ticket is a pass over it and you will not be given the budget to go looking twice.
      Then say, in a few sentences, what a rework of identity would have to preserve for the
      first half of this answer to keep meaning what it means today.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "10"
        required: true
        description: The identity lifecycle traced through the routines that establish, cache, seed and tear it down, each located at file:line, plus the shape of the population a change to that type would land on - the areas it reaches, near edge and far side, named once each with one located example
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_graph
        required: false
        layer: adoption
  - name: Audit every dependent of the server contract
    prompt: >
      Task: this is the core of the ticket, and you are working it with the budget the first
      part already cost you. You already established the reach of that one type as a set, in one
      go, and that set is a fixed thing with an edge to it - it is not going to grow now. This
      part is a pass straight down it, in the order you established it, with a judgement at each
      line inside it. It is not a second search, and starting again with fresh words is how this
      gets half done. Take it as settled that the whole record is being replaced, and answer the
      only question that matters after that: what breaks.
      Not the code that is handed one of these machines and works with it while it has it in
      front of it. Whatever share of the product that runs, it will be handed the new record and
      it will be fine, and it is not what I am asking for. What breaks is every place where the
      machine itself is not there and one identifying piece of it is - a piece lifted off a
      machine at some earlier moment by something else, and left somewhere the machine could
      never have been carried to.
      There are two fates for such a piece, and handing me only the first is how this part is
      failed. Either the piece comes back: it was parked in something that outlasts the moment,
      and later that same piece is turned back into a machine, and from there on the code trusts
      it to still mean the machine it came from. Or the piece never comes back at all: it was
      written in as a value of something else, or it was handed outside this product, and there
      is no lookup anywhere for the rework to break - what breaks instead is that the piece now
      says something untrue about which machine this is, and nothing here can reach it to
      correct it. Both fates are exactly what this rework withdraws, and not one of those places
      changes by a single line when it does. The second fate is the half this pass usually
      loses, because nothing there looks like a lookup and nothing there fails loudly; go and
      get it.
      Test each candidate at the line itself, and only there, and the test has two halves. Is a
      piece being lifted off a machine here and put somewhere the machine itself cannot be
      carried to - held, written in, or handed out? Or is a piece being read back out of
      somewhere, after the machine was gone? Either one is in. If instead a machine came in and
      the code acted on it while it had it, that is the ground, and the ground is out.
      One thing about how these places hide, because it decides how you work: almost none of
      them writes the name of that type anywhere near the line that matters. The piece was
      taken off a machine by something else, handed on, and by the time it lands here it is a
      plain value sitting in the middle of a job about something else entirely - so the name
      you fixed in the first part will not lead you to them, and looking for it harder will not
      either. They are inside the reach you already established; they are the part of it where
      the machine itself did not travel.
      How this part is judged, because it is not by how good the places you hand me are. It is
      judged on whether the pass reached the end of the set. Every member of the set you
      established gets a ruling out of you, and there are exactly two rulings it can get. Either
      a piece is being kept there, and it gets the four parts below in full; or it is the
      ground, and it gets its location and the few words that say why it is the ground, and
      nothing more - that side is meant to be cheap, so spend almost nothing on it. A member you
      simply passed over in silence is not an omission, it is a wrong answer, because from here
      it cannot be told apart from one you never reached at all. And do not filter by how sure
      you are: a member you are uncertain about is ruled with the doubt written beside it, never
      dropped. Handing me a short list of the ones you were confident about, with the rest of
      the set unaccounted for, is the specific way this part gets failed - as is closing while a
      member of it is still unruled and you still have working time in front of you.
      For each kept place the answer has four parts and only four: the exact file:line where
      the piece is taken, kept, handed out or read back, and the routine that line sits in;
      THE OTHER END OF THE HAND-OFF, which is the exact file:line in a different routine
      where that same piece was lifted off an actual machine before it ever arrived here, and
      the routine that line sits in - if the place you are ruling is itself where the lifting
      happens, then the other end is the line elsewhere that later reads the piece back, and
      if you can genuinely find no other end, say which of the two ends is missing and why
      rather than leaving the pair half-written; which of the two fates it is, and with it how
      far the piece outlives the machine it came from - inside one request, inside one stored
      record, or outside this product entirely and past correcting from here; and one phrase on
      what specifically starts being wrong the first time that piece stops meaning the machine
      it came from, saying plainly whether anything raises there or whether nothing does and it
      simply carries on. Keep it short: I want the coverage, the pair, the fate, the lifetime
      and the failure, not the prose.
      The pair is the point and it is not decoration. One end alone does not tell me whether
      anything still resolves: a line holding a bare value tells me nothing about which machine
      the value was taken from, or under what conditions, or whether the thing that took it is
      itself changing. A place given with only its own line is half a dependency and is worth
      half a place to me. And the two ends are almost never near each other - they sit in
      different areas, in code with different jobs, written by different people at different
      times, and the value travels between them by being handed on rather than by being named -
      so the ends of a pair cannot be read off each other and each one has to be established
      where it lives.
      Present the kept places organised by area, one section per area, and treat an area as
      finished only when every place inside it is listed separately with both ends of its pair
      located; then close with the ruled-out members in set order, one line each. If you
      genuinely run out of time, say where in the set you stopped and what was still below that
      point, rather than closing on what you have. A file:line with no other end, no fate and no
      failure is a fragment of an answer, and a group summarised instead of listed reads as a
      group with nothing in it.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "6"
        required: true
        description: A ruling on every member of the population established in the first part - each identity-keeping place given as a PAIR of located lines, the one where the piece is kept, written out or read back and the one in a different routine where it was lifted off an actual machine, each with the routine it sits in, plus which fate its piece has, how long it outlives the machine, and what that place gets wrong once the piece no longer means the same machine, with each ruled-out member accounted for in one line rather than passed over
      - type: no_grep
        value: no_grep
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption


scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.7
    efficiency: 0.3

gold:
  # ---- ANCHORS: the contract centre, its identity map, and its removal path. The answer to
  # ---- step 1. Both arms reach these and they do NOT score. Every line below was opened and
  # ---- read this session. None of these files appears in `dependents`.
  - id: contract-model
    group: contract
    match: [Models/Server.php]
    relation: "app/Models/Server.php:230 `public static function findCached($id): ?static` - identity resolved out of a per-request identity map keyed on the machine's number, flushed by :222 `static::flushIdentityMap();` inside the `static::updated(...)` closure opened at :221, with the flush itself defined at :251 `public static function flushIdentityMap(): void`; the identity fields themselves are what every scored row keeps a piece of"
  - id: c:server-setting
    group: contract
    match: [Models/ServerSetting.php]
    relation: "app/Models/ServerSetting.php:59 `'server_id',` in the fillable list and :249 `return $this->belongsTo(Server::class);` inside `public function server()` defined at :247 - the companion record seeded from a freshly created machine, holding the machine's number rather than the machine; the obvious first hop off the contract and reached by both arms in every cycle"
  - id: c:server-delete
    group: contract
    match: [Server/Delete.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Server/Delete.php:34 `public function delete($password, $selectedActions = [])` ending at :60 `$this->server->delete();` - the removal path, the moment after which every piece kept elsewhere resolves to nothing"


  # ---- DEPENDENTS: the scored group. THIRTEEN FILES, ELEVEN AREAS, one row per file, none
  # ---- of them in the `contract` group, every one present in the shown blast payload.
  # ---- SEVEN carried over from sha256:7e71ec7ad6b1c084, SIX new; the five rows the pooled
  # ---- ranking and the a23 baseline transcript agree are bought by one property sweep, or
  # ---- are dead on both arms, are cut. At every scored line there is no machine in hand:
  # ---- only a piece taken off one earlier and kept somewhere the machine cannot follow.

  # -- area: shared project screens
  - id: d:logs-ownership-check
    group: dependents
    match: [Project/Shared/GetLogs.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Project/Shared/GetLogs.php:127 `if (! Server::ownedByCurrentTeam()->where('id', $this->server->id)->exists()) {` opening `getLogs($refresh = false)` at :125 - a screen that holds a machine across requests throws it away and re-authorises by its number on every poll, so the permission decision rides on the number and not on the object it came from"

  # -- area: shared destination screens
  - id: d:additional-network-pivot
    group: dependents
    match: [Project/Shared/Destination.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Project/Shared/Destination.php:130 `$this->resource->additional_networks()->attach($mainDestination->id, ['server_id' => $mainDestination->server->id]);` with the paired `->wherePivot('server_id', $server->id)` at :128 - the number written into a join row as a column of the join itself, read back later to decide which extra machines a resource runs on"

  # -- area: resource creation wizard
  - id: d:new-resource-state-id
    group: dependents
    match: [Project/New/Select.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Project/New/Select.php:409 `$this->server_id = $server->id;` inside `setServer(Server $server)` at :407 - the number kept in the wizard's own state and rehydrated on every subsequent request, long after the machine object at :410 is gone"

